Here's how to implement all three requirements:

## 1. Intercept Back Navigation with a Confirmation Dialog

Override `OnBackButtonPressed` in your page:

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
            return base.OnBackButtonPressed();

        // Fire-and-forget the async dialog; return true to cancel navigation
        Dispatcher.Dispatch(async () =>
        {
            bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                "Unsaved Changes",
                "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
                "Discard", "Stay");

            if (leave)
            {
                _hasUnsavedChanges = false;
                await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            }
        });

        return true; // cancel the default back navigation
    }
}
```

> **Note:** `OnBackButtonPressed` handles the **hardware/OS back button** (Android back gesture, etc.). It does **not** fire when the user taps the Shell navbar back arrow.

## 2. Intercept the Shell Navbar Back Arrow

Shell doesn't expose a direct "navigation bar back tapped" event. The standard approach is to **replace it with a custom back button**—which also satisfies your third requirement:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.EditProductPage"
             Shell.BackButtonBehavior="{x:Null}">

    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior
            TextOverride="Cancel"
            Command="{Binding BackCommand}" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

## 3. ViewModel with the Back Command

```csharp
public partial class EditProductViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    [ObservableProperty]
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    public ICommand BackCommand { get; }

    public EditProductViewModel()
    {
        BackCommand = new AsyncRelayCommand(OnBackRequested);
    }

    private async Task OnBackRequested()
    {
        if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            bool leave = await Shell.Current.DisplayAlert(
                "Unsaved Changes",
                "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
                "Discard", "Stay");

            if (!leave) return; // stay on page
        }

        await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    }
}
```

### Key points

| Concern | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Hardware/OS back button | `OnBackButtonPressed()` override |
| Navbar back arrow | `BackButtonBehavior` with a `Command` |
| Custom button text | `BackButtonBehavior.TextOverride="Cancel"` |
| Custom icon instead of text | `BackButtonBehavior.IconOverride="icon.png"` |

Both paths funnel through the same confirmation logic, so the user always gets the dialog regardless of how they try to leave.
